    id: 'method-non-strawman-project-comparison-2026-08-28',
    s: 'methodology',
    r: 'both',
    t: 'Non-strawman comparison between an external source and the project',
    b: `PURPOSE. Determine what an external source supports, refines, leaves untouched, or opposes in the project without changing either side to manufacture agreement or conflict.

UNIT FIRST. Compare a source claim with one identified project claim at the same scope, level, time, register, modality, and evidence status. Never compare a whole author with a whole project when the evidence concerns one clause. The source remains answerable on its own terms before project application.

AUTHORSHIP AND ADOPTION. Keep direct source language, source interpretation, user-authored project claims, adopted project canon, assistant inference, assistant safeguard, and unresolved question separate. Update, mutation, test-writing, packaging, or publication authority does not confer semantic authority. Repetition, successful implementation, continuation, silence, frustration, or a request to harden never lets the assistant adopt its own interpretation on the user's behalf.

HONEST RELATION TAXONOMY. Strict contradiction is one form of opposition and never the only one. Classify the compared claim as one of the following, with explicit scope. Full support means the source supplies the material premises of the scoped project claim. Scoped support means it supports only an identified part. Compatible refinement adds a distinction that preserves the claim while making it more exact. Neutral compatible broadening covers a wider field without supporting or opposing the claim. Neutral partial overlap means the two claims share some cases while each also contains cases the other does not. Scoped partial compatibility means one bounded portion can coexist while another remains undecided or differently organized. Neutral difference or noncoverage means the source does not address the project claim. Failed adequate test means a source-grounded test was capable of bearing on the claim and the claim failed it. Negative evidence means the expected evidence is absent under adequate observability, with the inference limited to that evidence channel. Partial opposition means the source undermines one component while leaving another intact. Substantive incompatibility means both positions can be understood without literal negation yet cannot jointly govern the same object, scope, and case. Strict contradiction means one asserts what the other denies under the same conditions.

SET RELATIONS. Treat the candidate as the proposition being tested against the target. Equality of the relevant case sets can support equivalence at the tested scope. Candidate proper subset of target is refinement. Target proper subset of candidate is compatible broadening. Disjoint sets may support opposition only when both purport to classify the same object and scope. Overlap without containment is neutral partial overlap unless another premise establishes scoped partial compatibility or opposition. Candidate cases outside the target never erase support for every target case that is actually established.

RELAY AND GENEALOGY. A documented relay can oppose and defeat a scoped claim of independent arrival. It cannot automatically defeat the structural comparison, the project's general comparative method, or every other independence claim. Structural counterpart, historical genealogy, shared ancestry, translation traffic, and direct borrowing are separate relations.

CURRENT DETIENNE RULINGS. Detienne is an explicit point in favor of the project's always-already account because the user ruled it so. The support concerns comparability and relations becoming visible through comparison without needing the project's later name in advance. Do not demote this ruling to mere compatibility. Do not inflate it into total agreement, direct ancestry, or proof of every Polycognate. The narrower genealogy concern does not go against the project as presented. Its status is nonopposition as presented until a specific relay and a specific independence claim are paired. Nonopposition is not automatically support, compatibility, or adoption.

DEFECT PROVENANCE. Before reporting an objection as a substantive problem in the user's work or project, trace the attacked claim to its author. If the objection defeats only an assistant-authored paraphrase, bridge, premise, or extension, classify it as the assistant's rhetorical or interpretive defect and remove it from the substantive-objection list. A substantive objection survives only when it reaches a user-authored or adopted claim in its strongest actual form. A critic supplies pressure and evidence. The critic never receives automatic authority over the project or over the distinction between substantive and assistant-created defects.

OUTPUT. State the source claim, the project claim, both provenance states, the exact relation, the scope, the evidence, what survives, and what remains unratified. If the source raises no genuine challenge after ironmanning, say that nothing against the project remains. Do not preserve a defeated objection merely to produce balance.`,
